00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:04.470 align:start position:0%
an ammunitions depot has exploded in  crimea a week after similar blasts  rocked a russian air base on the annex  peninsula the russian defense ministry  originally attributed the blasts to a  fire at a military warehouse but now  says the damage is the result of  sabotage local officials say two people  were injured while another 2 000 were  evacuated as a precaution  ukraine has neither confirmed nor denied  its involvement in the recent blasts in  crimea the peninsula was illegally  annexed by russia in 2014.  dw correspondent down phillip schultz is  in keefe this isn't the first such blast  in crimea what has kiev been saying  about these incidents  of course hardly anybody here  believes that it's just a strange series  of accidents and some ukrainian  officials have made comments that leave  some room for interpretation let's say  shortly after the explosions  presidential advisor tweeted that  invaders have to understand that there  is a high risk of death in occupied  territories for them  so hardly  anybody believes  in an accident and  for many ukrainians  crimea is one of the  most symbolic places of this war they  would welcome any possible attack on  russian facilities there as you know  crimea has been occupied by russia for  the past eight years and president  zelinski has repeatedly made it clear  that any efforts to win the war any  effort to take back occupied territory  must include crimea  concerns are also growing about the  strikes near the safaris nuclear plant  what's the latest there  yes local authorities say that artillery  attacks have continued very close to the  plant in the past 48 hours that is very  concerning and they  say  that the russian side says that the  ukrainians are responsible for the  attacks and vice versa they blame  each other as usual  but we were able to talk to some  engineers that worked  at the plant until recently and they  confirm that the situation is getting  increasingly alarming a lot of  facilities have been destroyed in recent  days in theory both ukraine and russia  agree that there should be  a mission of international inspectors  but they disagree on the conditions  russia rejects any mission that travels  through uh non-occupied ukrainian uh  territory so at the moment there  is a little uh sign for improvements uh  unfortunately that is unfortunate young  philip schultz thank you very much for  bringing us up to date  on the situation in ukraine
 

